Keester Stacking Issue search — purpose and behavior
Purpose: finds log events from many network devices (switches/hosts) at the MAHG site that include the term "Stack" (i.e., stacking/system messages) so we can monitor stack‑related issues across the site.
What it does: searches the linux-server index for events from the program source "Stack" (sourcetype pixisyslog) across a long list of hosts at the MAHG site, then filters and sorts results by source and timestamp. The query surfaces recent stacking system messages (errors, status changes) for each specified device.
Why it’s useful: Stack issues (physical or logical stack failures) can cause connectivity loss or degraded service across multiple switch members. This search gives a focused view of all stack events so the ops team can quickly see where stacking problems are occurring and when.
Output example: device name, event text, and time — allowing engineers to triage stacking faults and correlate with other alerts.
Keesler Cluster Down Alerts search — purpose and behavior
Purpose: detect and report cluster/service‑level failures at the MAHG site by looking for cluster‑related log messages that indicate remote clients are down.
What it does: searches the linux server index for events from the CLUSTER program (sourcetype shown) on the MAHG hosts, looking for the phrase "Remote client CCEP down". It then sorts results by event time. The query surfaces recent cluster down messages so incident responders know which cluster nodes or remote clients are failing.
Why it’s useful: Cluster outages or services reporting remote clients down can indicate broader service interruptions or degraded application availability. This search quickly identifies affected hosts and times so the incident team can begin troubleshooting (network, service, or client issues).
Output example: host, timestamp, and the cluster error line — useful for creating notables/alerts or feeding incident tickets.
